ver the whole complexity of it yeah  you're transferring was you know  transferring was was a challenge was a  physical challenge I enjoyed it for many  years until it began to tear apart my  shoulders but anyway Tom we ended up in  the back yard and we ended up working  together and I was very impressed that  he chose to visit me for the first time  with his wheelchair and in that manner  and we were working out in the back yard  and I was trying to teach Tom how to do  wheelies in the backyard  and I remember popping I'm in a lecture  in an electric wheelchair now and I was  in a manual wheelchair up until you know  37 years up and I've only been in the  electric wheel  in the last couple of months but I was  still able to do wheelies this is this  is a bit too heavy to do wheelies now  but I'm instructing Tom in the backyard  and how to do wheelies well I just want  to give an example of Tom's great  courage  tom is Tom's just-just-just wants to  jump into everything you know he's not  afraid of anything and very courageous  and so he he threw himself into this and  so he's trying to Tom's trying to do  wheelie and and all of a sudden he goes  over backwards and there were these red  bricks in the in the backyard and he  went over backwards and his head bounced  like a ball you know just when he went  backwards and he went backwards and his  head bounced and and he I thought it  would have knocked him out I mean I was  but Tom came up smiling you know smiling  and laughing he just Tom just started  laughing you know and I just I knew we  had a real winner there you know again  Ron we just uh we're going to end just  in a few minutes but I just uh I told  you not to give speeches bro this little  game going but I I do want to ask you a  final question you know let's say having  observed you and how like I called  around the other day because I was  incredibly moved by the introduction I  really urge everyone to buy this book  give it to friends but I tell Ron I had  the book and I read the introduction and  I you know just flipped me out and so I  said he said well I haven't seen it yet  so I said well I'll come down there and  give you a copy so Ratana beach and he  said oh no he said I'm just getting  started  and then he described what he had to do  to get going in the morning and it's not  kidding around you know it's a real  routine it's really difficult and and so  you know we talked earlier about it you  talked about in the book you know not  giving in to despair not becoming angry  develop you know keeping and developing  your sense of humor you know and the  sort of positive and in the movie you're  really bitter you rip out the lines yell  and you tell me and I've seen it in our  connection how you've become you know  much more positive about life and so  forth and is actually  might want to read the last paragraph of  the book or the introductory I think  it's important to realize that let me  share this with you today that you know  we all not just myself all of us in this  room and all of us we all experienced  tragedy those of us that choose to keep  on living we experience loss and tragedy  and grief and we all will and we all  will be knocked down and have to pick  ourselves up and get back up again  and I I am so grateful to be here and to  be alive I am so so thankful to be able  to have found meaning in my life my  commitment to peace and non-violence my  commitment to trying to make this world  a better place and my commitment to just  each day trying to be a good and decent  human being just trying to be a good  neighbor trying to be kind  I really enjoyed writing the  introduction it meant a lot to me  because for years I felt I had written  the poem and I am the living death your  Memorial Day on wheels I am the living  death you Memorial Dan wheels and I had  written from my heart and I had I had  written about some of the worst of what  I had seen what I experienced I wanted  that war to end I didn't