leasure working  with him he's a wonderful wonderful  wonderful human being and it I just want  you to know was an honor putting this  new addition together with you and I  think it's beautiful and I'm grateful  thank you this is a let me read this  this this year this is the the end of  the the the introduction and and this is  something I I've wanted to share for a  long time I guess and let's see if I can  read this quickly I'm no longer the 28  year old man six years returned from the  war in Vietnam who sat behind that  typewriter in Santa Monica in the fall  of 1974 I am nearly 60 now my hair and  beard are almost completely white the  nightmares and anxiety attacks have all  but disappeared but I still do not sleep  well at night I toss and turn an  increasing physical pain but I remain  very positive and optimistic I still am  determined to rise above all of this I  know my pain and the horrors of my past  will always be with me  but perhaps not with the same force and  fury of those early years after the war  I have learned to forgive my enemies and  forgive myself it has been very  difficult to heal from the war while  living in America and I have often  dreamed of moving to neutral ground  another country yet I have somehow made  a certain peace even with a nation that  so often still seems to believe in war  and the use of violence as a solution to  its problems there has been a reckoning  a renewal the scar will always be there  a living reminder of that war  but it has also become something  beautiful now something of faith and  hope and love I've been given the  opportunity to move through that dark  night of the soul to a new Shore to gain  an understanding and knowledge an  entirely different vision I now believe  I have suffered for a reason and in many  ways I have found that reason in my  commitment to peace and non-violence my  life has been a blessing in disguise  even with the pain and great difficulty  that my physical disability continues to